\section{Finding the learnlog directory}
\label{sec:find-learnlog-dir}

Before creating a new repository, we search upward from the current
directory for an existing [[.learnlog/]] directory, mirroring how Git
itself searches for [[.git/]].
This lets a student work across multiple lab subdirectories while all
activity is recorded in a single repository at the course root.
The function lives in the core module because both import-time
initialisation and the CLI need it.

Where the search \emph{stops} turns out to matter more than where it
starts, and the first version had no stopping rule at all: it walked
to the filesystem root.
That is not the harmless generalisation it looks like.
One unanchored run launched from a home directory is enough to create
a [[.learnlog/]] there, and from then on every unanchored run anywhere
below it resolves to \emph{that} repository---whose worktree is the
whole home directory, which [[begin_run]] then stages with
[[git add .]] (\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}).
The observed result was a 1.7~GB repository built out of one stray
directory.
The damage grows with how far the walk is allowed to travel, so the
walk needs a boundary.

We use two, and both say the same thing: the search may look for the
project a run belongs to, but it may not wander out of it.

\begin{description}
\item[The home directory] The home directory and everything above it
  is never a project root.
  A home directory is where projects live rather than being one, so
  adopting it makes every unrelated file in it part of one student's
  log.
\item[The enclosing Git working tree] A directory holding a [[.git]]
  is where the search stops when it has no [[.learnlog/]] of its own.
  It is the conventional marker for \enquote{this is a self-contained
  project}, so a [[.learnlog/]] further up belongs to something else.
\end{description}

Within a directory we look for [[.learnlog/]] \emph{before} asking
whether we have reached a boundary, and that order is what keeps the
ordinary layout working: a course root that is both a Git checkout and
a learnlog project is still found from a lab subdirectory below it.

The cost we accept is the opposite nesting.
A Git checkout \emph{inside} a learnlog project---a per-lab repository
cloned into a course directory---no longer resolves to the course
root, so an unanchored run there starts a log of its own.
We prefer that trade because the two outcomes are not comparable in
severity: a split log is a nuisance a teacher can merge, while an
adopted home directory is an unbounded commit of unrelated files.
The [[learnlog init]] workflow does not reach this code at all---a
venv wired by [[init]] anchors the run through its marker file
(\cref{sec:autostart})---so this walk is only ever the guess made when
there is nothing to anchor to.

<<functions>>=
def _is_home_or_above(path, home=None):
    """True iff ``path`` is the home directory or an ancestor of it.

    Such a directory is never a learnlog project root: it holds
    projects rather than being one.
    """
    try:
        home = pathlib.Path(
            home if home is not None else pathlib.Path.home()
        ).resolve()
        path = pathlib.Path(path).resolve()
    except Exception:
        return False
    return path == home or path in home.parents
@

An unusable home directory---no [[HOME]], no matching password entry,
a path we cannot resolve---makes the predicate answer [[False]] rather
than raise.
Failing open is the right direction here: the worst case is the old
unbounded behaviour, whereas failing closed would refuse to find a
perfectly ordinary project because of an unrelated environment
problem.

Applying the two boundaries at every step gives the search itself.

<<functions>>=
def find_learnlog_dir(start=None):
    """Walk up from ``start`` to find a ``.learnlog/`` directory.

    The search never considers the home directory or anything above
    it, and it stops at the root of the enclosing Git working tree.
    Returns the directory containing ``.learnlog/``, or ``None`` if
    no learnlog repository is found within those bounds.
    """
    path = pathlib.Path(start or os.getcwd()).resolve()
    while not _is_home_or_above(path):
        if (path / ".learnlog").is_dir():
            return path
        if (path / ".git").exists():
            return None
        parent = path.parent
        if parent == path:
            return None
        path = parent
    return None
@

The Git boundary is tested with [[exists]] rather than [[is_dir]] on
purpose.
In a linked worktree, a submodule, or any checkout produced by
[[git worktree add]], [[.git]] is a \emph{file} holding a [[gitdir:]]
line---and those are checkouts the walk must not escape either.

Let's verify that the upward search works correctly.
These tests use [[LearnlogRepo]] (\cref{gitrepo}) to create a
[[.learnlog/]] directory in an isolated temporary directory:

<<test functions>>=
class TestFindLearnlogDir:
    def test_find_in_current(self, workdir):
        """find_learnlog_dir returns the directory
        containing .learnlog/."""
        from learnlog import find_learnlog_dir
        LearnlogRepo(workdir)
        assert find_learnlog_dir(workdir) == workdir

    def test_find_from_subdirectory(self, workdir):
        """find_learnlog_dir walks up to find
        .learnlog/ in a parent."""
        from learnlog import find_learnlog_dir
        LearnlogRepo(workdir)
        sub = workdir / "subdir"
        sub.mkdir()
        assert find_learnlog_dir(sub) == workdir

    def test_returns_none_when_missing(self, tmp_path):
        """find_learnlog_dir returns None if no
        .learnlog/ exists."""
        from learnlog import find_learnlog_dir
        assert find_learnlog_dir(tmp_path) is None
@

Now the two boundaries.
The home test reproduces the incident in miniature: a [[.learnlog/]]
sitting in the home directory, and a run one level below it that must
\emph{not} find it.
The Git test pairs the two nestings against each other, which is the
only way to see that the boundary is applied after the [[.learnlog/]]
check rather than before it: the same [[.git]] marker stops the search
in one layout and is ignored in the other.

