\section{Initialisation}
\label{sec:initialize}

The [[initialize]] function is called at import time.
It detects the main script, creates (or opens) the learnlog repository,
records the run header, and installs the I/O wrappers.

Why detect the main script via [[sys.argv[0]]] rather than
[[__file__]]?
Because [[__file__]] gives us the file that contains the import
statement, which might be a library module.
We want the script the student actually invoked from the command line.

The entire function is wrapped in a [[try]]/[[except]] that catches
\emph{everything}.
If learnlog fails for any reason---no Git installed, permission denied,
disk full---the student's program must still work.

This handler is the last line of defence, and it stays one: nothing that
happens here may reach the student's program, and it must keep catching
[[Exception]] rather than the [[GitError]] of \cref{sec:diagnostics}
alone, since \emph{any} failure in learnlog is learnlog's problem and
not the program's.
What changes is where the exception goes.
Discarding it made a failed run indistinguishable from a successful one
for everybody---including us---so instead of [[pass]] we hand it to
[[report_error]], which records it somewhere that costs the running
program nothing and stays silent unless [[LEARNLOG_DEBUG]] asks
otherwise.

Autostart widens the set of invocations that reach [[initialize]].
Besides ordinary [[script.py]] runs we now see the bare REPL,
[[python -c]], [[python -m]], and entry-point scripts such as
[[pip]] or [[pytest]].
Those forms need two normalisations before we write a run header:
which repository to anchor to, and what human-readable name to give
the invocation.

The repository lookup therefore has three sources, tried in order of
decreasing authority: an inherited [[LEARNLOG_PROJECT_ROOT]], the venv
anchor resolved by [[_resolve_project_root]] (\cref{sec:autostart}),
and finally the upward search from the current directory.
The venv anchor is what lets a script launched from [[/tmp]] still log
into the course project's [[.learnlog/]] instead of dropping a fresh
repository next to whatever the student happened to [[cd]] into.
There is also a fourth possible answer, \enquote{nowhere}: some
invocations must not be logged at all rather than logged in the wrong
place, and [[_resolve_workdir]] says so by raising.

The invocation name is normalised rather than copied from
[[sys.argv[0]]] verbatim.
Raw values such as the empty string or [[-c]] are faithful to CPython,
but poor commit headers for humans.
We map those sentinel forms to labels that explain how Python was
started while leaving ordinary filenames and entry-point basenames
unchanged.

For [[python -c]] there is a second wrinkle: real interpreters do not
keep the source in [[sys.argv]].
At runtime [[sys.argv]] starts with [[-c]] and then contains only the
student's extra arguments, while [[sys.orig_argv]] still remembers the
full interpreter command line including the source string.
If we only inspect [[sys.argv]], explicit-import tests can pass while the
real autostart path still records [[Arguments: (none)]]---and, worse,
[[python -c "code" extra]] would record [[extra]] as if it were the
source.
We therefore let the normaliser consult [[sys.orig_argv]] first when it
recognises the [[-c]] sentinel: that path recovers the real source from
[[sys.orig_argv[2]]] and preserves the student's extra arguments from
[[sys.argv[1:]]].
The bare [[sys.argv]] fast path remains only as a fallback for callers
that do not supply [[sys.orig_argv]].

Before any of that, [[initialize]] must decide whether this invocation
should be logged \emph{at all}.
Two conditions veto it: the [[LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART]] escape hatch,
and the [[learnlog init]] bootstrap invocation, which is busy creating
the repository and so has none to commit into yet.

The veto has to be evaluated \emph{here} rather than in the startup
hook, and the reason is subtle enough that it caused a real bug: the
hook's checks were dead code for as long as they existed.
The [[.pth]] line reads \mintinline{python}{import learnlog._autostart},
and Python imports a package before any of its submodules.
By the time [[learnlog._autostart.autostart()]] is called,
[[learnlog/__init__.py]] has already run to completion---including
[[initialize]], including the commit that starts the run.
A gate consulted from [[autostart()]] is asked its question after the
answer has been acted upon.
\cref{sec:autostart-import-order} works through the consequences from
the hook's side.

Putting the veto in [[initialize]] also covers the paths the hook never
sees at all.
The installed CLI entry point imports [[learnlog.cli]], which imports
[[learnlog]] through ordinary package loading; a student can write
\mintinline{python}{import learnlog} at the top of a script; there is
also [[python -c "import learnlog"]] and the REPL.
[[initialize]] is the one place every such path passes through, and the
only place where the run is actually started---so it is the only place
where a veto can be complete.

That makes [[LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART]] a \emph{hard} opt-out: it
suppresses logging on every import path, not merely on the [[.pth]]
one.
The widened meaning is what the variable was always supposed to have.
An escape hatch that only silences one of four entry points cannot be
used by an outer layer that has taken responsibility for a run, and
cannot reliably keep learnlog's own test suite from logging the very
runs it is testing.

<<functions>>=
def _is_opted_out(argv, env=None):
    """True iff this invocation must not be logged at all.

    Covers the ``LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART`` escape hatch and the
    ``learnlog init`` bootstrap invocation.
    """
    if env is None:
        env = os.environ
    if env.get("LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART"):
        return True
    return _is_learnlog_init(argv)
@

Detecting the bootstrap invocation is a matter of reading [[argv]].
[[argv[0]]] is either the [[learnlog]] entry-point script installed in
the venv's [[bin/]], or a path to the package's [[__main__.py]] when
the student ran [[python -m learnlog init]]; both carry [[learnlog]] as
a path component, which is why one predicate covers both spellings.
Only [[init]] is excluded---every other [[learnlog]] subcommand is
research-valuable data about how a student interacts with their own log
and is captured like any other program.

<<functions>>=
def _is_learnlog_init(argv):
    """True iff ``argv`` looks like a ``learnlog init`` invocation.

    Recognises the installed ``learnlog`` entry-point script and
    ``python -m learnlog`` (in which case ``argv[0]`` contains
    ``learnlog`` as a path component).
    """
    if len(argv) < 2 or argv[1] != "init":
        return False
    basename = os.path.basename(argv[0])
    if basename == "learnlog":
        return True
    return "learnlog" in pathlib.PurePath(argv[0]).parts
@

Let's verify that the veto fires for exactly the invocations it should.
The ordinary shapes---a script, the bare REPL, [[python -c]],
[[python -m]], console entry points, and non-[[init]] [[learnlog]]
subcommands---must all pass through, while the two spellings of
[[learnlog init]] and the escape hatch must all stop.

<<test functions>>=
class TestIsOptedOut:
    @pytest.mark.parametrize("argv", [
        ["script.py"],
        [""],
        ["-c", "1+1"],
        ["-m", "foo"],
        ["/venv/bin/pip", "install", "foo"],
        ["/venv/bin/pytest", "--version"],
        ["learnlog", "play"],
        ["learnlog", "export"],
    ])
    def test_ordinary_invocations_are_logged(self, argv):
        assert not _is_opted_out(argv, env={})

    def test_skip_env_var_vetoes_any_invocation(self):
