<<functions>>=
def _normalize_main_invocation(argv, orig_argv=None):
    """Return a normalised ``(script, args)`` pair from ``argv``."""
    argv = list(argv) if argv else [""]
    orig_argv = list(orig_argv) if orig_argv else []
    script = argv[0]
    special = {
        "": ("<repl>", lambda values: []),
        "-m": ("<python -m>", lambda values: values[1:]),
    }
    if script == "-c":
        if len(orig_argv) > 2 and orig_argv[1] == "-c":
            return "<python -c>", [orig_argv[2], *argv[1:]]
        if len(argv) > 1:
            return "<python -c>", [argv[1]]
        return "<python -c>", []
    if script in special:
        name, args_factory = special[script]
        return name, args_factory(argv)
    basename = os.path.basename(script)
    if script.endswith(".py"):
        return basename, argv[1:]
    return basename, argv[1:]
@

A [[python -c]] run differs from a normal script run in one important
way: the source exists only in the command-line argument.
If we keep that string only in the commit body, the run has no code diff
and downstream consumers such as the viewer or ProgSnap2 [[CodeState]]
exports cannot recover an authoritative program text.
We therefore materialise the source as a regular worktree file before
[[begin_run]] stages the tree.

The worktree is the right place for that file.
Writing inside [[.learnlog/]] would keep it out of the tracked project
tree, while adding more commit-message metadata would still leave
[[git log -p]] and checked-out code states empty.
This helper also belongs in [[learnlog.nw]] rather than [[gitrepo.nw]]:
only the Python-facing initialisation code knows that
[[<python -c>]] means [[args[0]]] is source code, whereas
[[LearnlogRepo]] should stay a generic Git wrapper.

\emph{Which} worktree is the part that is easy to get wrong, and
getting it wrong turns the record into litter.
The directory the student happens to stand in is not the answer: an
anchored run---one pointed at a project by [[LEARNLOG_PROJECT_ROOT]] or
by a venv that [[learnlog init]] wired---is logged into that project no
matter where it was launched from, so a file written next to the
student would land outside the tree [[begin_run]] stages and outside
the repository that records the run.
The helper therefore takes the [[LearnlogRepo]] instead of a directory
of its own and writes under [[repo.workdir]].
Passing the repository rather than a second copy of the path is what
makes the two impossible to disagree about: the file is written into
exactly the directory Git is about to commit from.

The file name is the second hazard.
A student may already own a [[python_c_invocation.py]], and overwriting
it would destroy their work---a far worse breach than the one we are
recording.
So learnlog writes only to a name it has claimed, and claims a name
only while nothing occupies it.
The claim is recorded in [[.learnlog/c-invocation]] rather than inside
the file.
It has to outlive the process, or the next [[python -c]] run would find
its own previous file in the way and claim yet another name; and it
must not be a marker line in the source file, because the bytes of that
file are the program that ran---a code state that quietly gained a
comment learnlog wrote is not a record of the student's code.

The trade-off is unchanged: [[python_c_invocation.py]] is visible in
[[ls]] after such a run.
That is acceptable because it is a discoverable record of the most
recent [[python -c]] source, in the project the run belongs to, and
because it stays one file that later runs update in place.
Failing to write it must not stop the student's program, but it must
not pass unnoticed either, so the exception we swallow goes to the
diagnostics path (\cref{sec:diagnostics}) like every other one.

<<functions>>=
def _persist_c_invocation(repo, script, args):
    """Persist ``python -c`` source as a worktree file."""
    if script != "<python -c>" or not args:
        return
    try:
        target = _claim_c_invocation_path(repo)
        if target is not None:
            target.write_text(args[0])
    except Exception as error:
        _report_swallowed_failure("persist -c source", error)
@

Claiming is a two-step lookup, and the order between the steps is what
keeps the name stable.
An existing claim wins outright, even when the file it names has since
been deleted: re-creating the name learnlog used last time turns
consecutive [[python -c]] runs into a one-file diff instead of a
growing pile of near-identical files.
Only when no claim exists do we go looking for a free name.

<<functions>>=
def _claim_c_invocation_path(repo):
    """Return the worktree path learnlog owns for ``-c`` sources."""
    claim = repo.git_dir / C_INVOCATION_CLAIM_NAME
    <<reuse the claimed name>>
    <<claim the first free name>>
@

A claim is a bare file name in the root of the work tree, so anything
carrying a separator is not a claim we wrote.
Discarding such a value instead of following it keeps a corrupted or
hand-edited [[c-invocation]] from redirecting the write to an arbitrary
path.

<<reuse the claimed name>>=
try:
    claimed = claim.read_text().strip()
except OSError:
    claimed = ""
if claimed and "/" not in claimed:
    return repo.workdir / claimed
@

The alternatives are numbered and tried in a fixed order, so a project
whose preferred name is taken still ends up with one stable file rather
than a new one per run.
The search is bounded because a directory in which a hundred of these
names are occupied is not one attempt away from success; there we
decline to write anything, which costs that run its synthetic code
state but touches nothing the student owns.

<<claim the first free name>>=
stem = C_INVOCATION_NAME.removesuffix(".py")
for attempt in range(C_INVOCATION_MAX_NAMES):
    candidate = C_INVOCATION_NAME
    if attempt:
        candidate = f"{stem}-{attempt}.py"
    if (repo.workdir / candidate).exists():
        continue
    claim.write_text(candidate + "\n")
    return repo.workdir / candidate
return None
<<constants>>=
C_INVOCATION_NAME = "python_c_invocation.py"
C_INVOCATION_CLAIM_NAME = "c-invocation"
C_INVOCATION_MAX_NAMES = 100
@

