The three-source lookup announced above is worth spelling out as its
own function, because each source answers a different question and the
order between them is the whole design.

The dividing line runs between the first two sources and the third.
[[LEARNLOG_PROJECT_ROOT]] and the venv anchor are \emph{statements}:
somebody---an outer layer, or the human who ran [[learnlog init]]---has
said which project this run belongs to.
The upward search is a \emph{guess} assembled from the working
directory.
Statements are obeyed as given, guesses are bounded
(\cref{sec:find-learnlog-dir}), and a guess we are not entitled to make
is refused outright.

[[LEARNLOG_PROJECT_ROOT]] comes first: if it is already in the
environment, some outer layer---a parent Python process, or a future
[[learnlog run]]---has decided, and an inner layer that overrode that
decision would split one session across two repositories.

The venv anchor comes second.
It is the answer for every run through a venv wired by [[learnlog
init]], and it is the source that makes the working directory
irrelevant: [[python /tmp/scratch.py]] belongs to the project the venv
was wired for, not to [[/tmp]].
When it answers, we export the result so that any child process
inherits the same anchor without having to re-resolve it---including
children started with a different interpreter, which would not run our
[[.pth]] hook at all.

