Both refusals travel as an exception rather than as a [[None]] return,
and that is not a stylistic choice.
[[initialize]] already wraps everything in the handler that turns
\enquote{learnlog cannot do its job} into a diagnostic which costs the
student's program nothing (\cref{sec:initialize}), and raising is what
routes the refusal through it: the run is abandoned before any stream
is wrapped, [[repo]] stays [[None]], and the reason is recorded by
[[report_error]] exactly like any other swallowed failure.
Since a refused run has no repository to write into, [[report_error]]
finds no [[errors.log]] to append to and the explanation surfaces only
under [[LEARNLOG_DEBUG]]---which is the whole story: there is nowhere
to write, so there is nothing written.

A distinct exception type is what keeps that entry readable.
[[errors.log]] and the debug mirror both print the class name, so
[[UnanchoredRun]] tells a reader that learnlog declined on purpose
rather than crashed.

