Let's verify that initialisation creates the repository and wraps
streams.
We run a script in a subprocess to avoid polluting the test process.
Those subprocesses import [[learnlog]] explicitly inside the test
script, so they must not also inherit the development venv's startup
hook: whichever project that venv happens to be wired to would anchor
the run, and the test's temporary directory would stay empty.

The isolation must not come from [[LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART]].
That variable is the hard opt-out [[initialize]] honours above, so
setting it would suppress the very [[import learnlog]] these tests are
checking.
More fundamentally, a test that disables the venv hook through
learnlog's own switch cannot distinguish \enquote{the hook stayed out
of the way} from \enquote{the switch is broken and the hook ran
anyway}---which is precisely the failure that hid this bug.
Every subprocess below therefore runs with [[python -S]], which makes
CPython skip [[site.py]] and with it all [[.pth]] processing, and gets
the package from an explicit [[PYTHONPATH]] instead of from
[[site-packages]].
The [[test_env]] fixture supplies that [[PYTHONPATH]] together with a
Git identity, so the tests also work in CI environments without a
global Git config.
It also strips both [[LEARNLOG_]] variables from the inherited
environment: a developer who exports the opt-out in their own shell
must not thereby turn these tests into vacuous passes.

These tests read the whole log rather than the last commit: a run is
recorded as a header commit and a trailer commit
(\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}), so the command and its results sit
in different commits.

