\section{Recording the exit status}
\label{sec:exit-status}

Every run's trailer carries an [[Exit-Code:]] line, and the analysis
tools turn it into a verdict: [[classify_run_block]]
(\cref{sec:classify-run-outcomes}) calls a run a failure when the code
is non-zero, and the ProgSnap2 exporter (\cref{progsnap2}) maps the
same field onto [[ProgramResult]].
A number we invent rather than observe therefore does not merely lose
detail---it mislabels the run.

The natural place to read the status is the exit handler, but the
interpreter never tells Python code which status it is about to use.
When an uncaught [[SystemExit]] reaches the top of the stack, CPython
does not consult [[sys.excepthook]] at all: it special-cases the
exception in C, reads its [[code]] attribute, converts it to an
integer, clears the exception, and calls [[Py_Exit]].
Interpreter finalisation---and with it our [[atexit]] handler---runs
\emph{inside} that call, by which time [[sys.exc_info()]] is empty
again and the integer exists only on the C side.
An exit handler that asks \enquote{how did this end?} can thus
distinguish nothing, which is why the earlier code had to guess: it
reported~[[0]] unless the exception hook had fired, so
\mintinline{python}{sys.exit(3)} was logged as a clean run and
exported as [[Success]].

The status has to be captured where it is still visible: at the moment
[[SystemExit]] is raised.
[[sys.exit]] is an ordinary attribute of the [[sys]] module, so we can
put a wrapper in front of it that notes the status and then delegates,
exactly as we already do for [[builtins.input]].

<<install exit status hook>>=
original_exit = sys.exit
sys.exit = _wrap_exit(original_exit)
@

The wrapper takes [[*args]] instead of a parameter defaulting to
[[None]] so that it can pass the call on unchanged.
\mintinline{python}{sys.exit()} must keep raising
\mintinline{python}{SystemExit()} with an empty [[args]] tuple rather
than the subtly different \mintinline{python}{SystemExit(None)}: a
program that catches its own [[SystemExit]] and inspects it must not
be able to tell that we are in the way.

Only the main thread's [[SystemExit]] ends the process.
A worker thread that calls [[sys.exit]] raises an exception that
[[threading.excepthook]] discards on purpose, so the thread stops and
the program carries on; recording that status would report a failure
for a run that goes on to succeed.
We therefore note the status only when the call comes from the thread
whose stack the interpreter will actually unwind.
The check lives in its own function because the wrapper is not the only
caller---the [[exit()]] built-in needs exactly the same treatment below.

<<functions>>=
def _record_system_exit(code):
    """Note the status a main-thread ``SystemExit`` will produce."""
    global system_exit_status
    if threading.current_thread() is threading.main_thread():
        system_exit_status = _system_exit_status(code)
@

[[functools.wraps]] copies the built-in's [[__name__]], [[__doc__]] and
[[__module__]] onto the wrapper, so [[help(sys.exit)]] and anything else
that introspects the function still describes the real one.

<<functions>>=
def _wrap_exit(original_exit):
    """Return a ``sys.exit`` wrapper that records the exit status."""
    @functools.wraps(original_exit)
    def logged_exit(*args):
        _record_system_exit(args[0] if args else None)
        original_exit(*args)

    return logged_exit
@

Let's verify that the wrapper still passes for the built-in it replaces:

<<test functions>>=
