\subsection{The [[exit()]] and [[quit()]] built-ins}

[[sys.exit]] is not the only door out.
Students who met Python in the REPL write \mintinline{python}{exit(1)}
in scripts too, and that name is not [[sys.exit]]: [[site]] installs a
[[Quitter]] instance in [[builtins]] which closes [[sys.stdin]] and then
raises [[SystemExit]] itself, never going near our wrapper.

A plain function cannot stand in for it.
Typing [[exit]] \emph{without} parentheses in the REPL is supposed to
answer \enquote{Use exit() or Ctrl-D (i.e. EOF) to exit}, and that
answer is [[Quitter.__repr__]] talking; a function would print its own
[[<function ...>]] instead and change what the student sees.
The stand-in is therefore an object that records the status, delegates
the call, and hands [[repr]] straight back to the original.

<<functions>>=
class _QuitterProxy:
    """Stand-in for the ``exit``/``quit`` built-ins.

    Records the exit status, then delegates to the original quitter.
    Attribute access that matters for these objects is ``repr``, which
    the REPL shows when the name is typed without parentheses; it comes
    from the original so the message is unchanged.
    """

    def __init__(self, original):
        self._original = original

    def __call__(self, code=None):
        _record_system_exit(code)
        self._original(code)

    def __repr__(self):
        return repr(self._original)
@

Let's verify both halves of that contract, the recording and the
borrowed [[repr]]:

<<test functions>>=
class FakeQuitter:
    """Stand-in for site.Quitter with its distinctive repr."""

    def __repr__(self):
        return "Use exit() or Ctrl-D (i.e. EOF) to exit"

    def __call__(self, code=None):
        raise SystemExit(code)


class TestQuitterProxy:
    def test_repr_comes_from_the_original(self):
        from learnlog import _QuitterProxy
        proxy = _QuitterProxy(FakeQuitter())
        assert repr(proxy) == repr(FakeQuitter())

    def test_call_records_status_and_delegates(self, monkeypatch):
        import learnlog
        monkeypatch.setattr(learnlog, "system_exit_status", None)
        proxy = learnlog._QuitterProxy(FakeQuitter())
        with pytest.raises(SystemExit):
            proxy(2)
        assert learnlog.system_exit_status == 2
@

Both names get the same treatment, and either may be missing---
[[python -S]] skips [[site.py]] altogether, and an embedded interpreter
may never create them---so we replace only what is there.
The function also reports whether it managed to wrap both, because the
caller has a second strategy for when it did not, and it must stay
harmless to call twice.

<<constants>>=
QUITTER_NAMES = ("exit", "quit")
<<functions>>=
def _install_quitter_proxies():
    """Wrap the ``exit``/``quit`` built-ins with recording proxies.

    Returns ``True`` once both names carry a proxy.
    """
    for name in QUITTER_NAMES:
        original = getattr(builtins, name, None)
        if original is None or isinstance(original, _QuitterProxy):
            continue
        setattr(builtins, name, _QuitterProxy(original))
    return all(
        isinstance(getattr(builtins, name, None), _QuitterProxy)
        for name in QUITTER_NAMES
    )
@


\subsection{When the built-ins do not exist yet}

There is an ordering trap here, and it bites in exactly the
configuration that matters most.
[[site.py]] evaluates [[.pth]] files inside [[addsitepackages()]] and
only \emph{afterwards} calls [[setquit()]] to create the two
[[Quitter]] instances.
Our autostart hook (\cref{sec:autostart}) is a [[.pth]] line, so in a
student's wired venv [[initialize]] runs before [[builtins.exit]]
exists, and a straightforward replacement would silently wrap nothing.
The symptom would be a feature that passes a hand-written test using an
explicit \mintinline{python}{import learnlog} and does nothing at all
for the students it was written for.

We therefore install the proxies as soon as the quitters appear.
The [[site]] module is halfway through its own [[main()]] when our hook
runs, so it is already in [[sys.modules]] and we can replace its
[[setquit]] with one that calls the original and then wraps what that
call has just created.
The replacement puts [[site.setquit]] back before delegating, so the
[[site]] module is left as we found it and a repeated call cannot stack
a second layer.
When learnlog is imported the ordinary way---from a script, long after
startup---[[setquit]] has already run, the first attempt succeeds, and
this path is never taken.

<<functions>>=
def _install_quitter_proxies_after_site(site_module):
    """Install the quitter proxies once ``site`` has created them."""
    original_setquit = getattr(site_module, "setquit", None)
    if original_setquit is None:
        return

    def setquit():
        site_module.setquit = original_setquit
        original_setquit()
        _install_quitter_proxies()

    site_module.setquit = setquit
@

<<install exit status hook>>=
if not _install_quitter_proxies():
    site_module = sys.modules.get("site")
    if site_module is not None:
        _install_quitter_proxies_after_site(site_module)
@

