\subsection{Translating a [[SystemExit]] code}

The [[code]] carried by a [[SystemExit]] is not an exit status yet.
We reproduce CPython's own translation, because the point of the
exercise is to predict what the process will do, not to invent a
second convention:
\begin{description}
\item[A [[None]] code] means success.
  Both \mintinline{python}{sys.exit()} and
  \mintinline{python}{sys.exit(None)} end the program normally, so they
  must be indistinguishable from falling off the end of the script.
\item[An integer] is the status itself.
  Booleans count as integers here, matching CPython's
  [[PyLong_Check]]: \mintinline{python}{sys.exit(False)} is a success
  and \mintinline{python}{sys.exit(True)} a failure, which is what makes
  the [[unittest]] idiom \mintinline{python}{sys.exit(not ok)} work.
\item[Any other object] means failure.
  CPython prints it to [[sys.stderr]] and exits with~1, so
  \mintinline{python}{sys.exit("no input file")} is a failing run whose
  message we capture as ordinary [[stderr]] output---the streams are
  still wrapped when the interpreter writes it.
\end{description}

The mask deserves a word.
[[Py_Exit]] hands its integer to the C library's [[exit]], which keeps
only the low eight bits, so the status a parent process can observe for
\mintinline{python}{sys.exit(300)} is~44 and for
\mintinline{python}{sys.exit(-1)} it is~255.
We record the truncated value because the log is a record of what
happened, and what happened is what the shell, the test runner, or a
future [[learnlog run]] wrapper would see.

<<constants>>=
EXIT_STATUS_MASK = 0xFF
<<functions>>=
def _system_exit_status(code):
    """Translate a ``SystemExit`` code into a process exit status.

    Mirrors CPython: ``None`` is success, an integer is the status
    truncated to the eight bits the operating system keeps, and any
    other object is printed to stderr and yields 1.
    """
    if code is None:
        return 0
    if isinstance(code, int):
        return code & EXIT_STATUS_MASK
    return 1
@

Let's verify the translation against the cases the prose distinguishes:

<<test functions>>=
class TestSystemExitStatus:
    def test_none_is_success(self):
        from learnlog import _system_exit_status
        assert _system_exit_status(None) == 0

    def test_integer_is_the_status(self):
        from learnlog import _system_exit_status
        assert _system_exit_status(3) == 3

    def test_booleans_count_as_integers(self):
        from learnlog import _system_exit_status
        assert _system_exit_status(False) == 0
        assert _system_exit_status(True) == 1

    def test_status_is_truncated_like_the_c_library(self):
        from learnlog import _system_exit_status
        assert _system_exit_status(-1) == 255
        assert _system_exit_status(300) == 44

    def test_other_objects_mean_failure(self):
        from learnlog import _system_exit_status
        assert _system_exit_status("no input file") == 1
        assert _system_exit_status(object()) == 1
@


\subsection{Choosing the status to record}

Two observations can now disagree, so the exit handler has to rank
them.
An unhandled exception wins over a recorded [[SystemExit]]: once the
exception hook has fired we know the traceback reached the top of the
stack, and CPython ends such a run with status~1 no matter what the
program did earlier.
This also repairs the one case the wrapper alone gets wrong---a program
that calls [[sys.exit]], catches its own [[SystemExit]], and then dies
of a real exception.
The remaining blind spot is the mirror image, a swallowed
[[sys.exit]] followed by a clean end; we accept it because a program
that asks to terminate and then carries on regardless is pathological,
whereas guessing~[[0]] for every deliberate exit got the ordinary case
wrong.

<<functions>>=
def _resolve_exit_code():
    """Return the status the interpreter will terminate with."""
    if exception_info is not None:
        return 1
    if system_exit_status is not None:
        return system_exit_status
    return 0
@

Let's verify the ranking, including that an unhandled exception
overrides a [[SystemExit]] the program swallowed on the way:

<<test functions>>=
