\subsection{What we cannot observe}

Three ways of ending a run never reach the wrapper, and it is worth
being explicit about what the log says for each.

\begin{description}
\item[Bypassing shutdown with [[os._exit]] ] terminates the process
  through the C library without finalising the interpreter, so no
  [[atexit]] handler runs and the run keeps the header
  [[begin_run]] committed but never gets a trailer.
  A block with no [[Exit-Code:]] line is classified as \emph{unknown}
  rather than as a failure (\cref{sec:classify-run-outcomes}), which
  is the honest answer: we know the run started and nothing about how
  it ended.
  We deliberately leave [[os._exit]] alone.  Its whole purpose is to
  skip cleanup---a forked child that must not run its parent's
  handlers depends on that---so wrapping it would change program
  behaviour rather than observe it.
\item[Death by signal] cannot be seen from inside at all.
  [[SIGKILL]] is not deliverable to a handler, and catching
  [[SIGTERM]] would not be observation either: a Python-level handler
  turns an immediate, default-disposition kill into an ordinary
  interpreter shutdown, changes the status the parent reads unless the
  handler carefully restores the default disposition and re-raises,
  and either overwrites a handler the student's own program installs
  or is overwritten by it.
  Since transparency outranks completeness here, we install no signal
  handlers, and such runs stay headers without trailers as well.
  A real wait status can only be read by whatever waits for the
  process, which is why exit-status fidelity for killed runs belongs
  to a future [[learnlog run]] wrapper rather than to this module.
\item[Raising [[SystemExit]] directly] is the one remaining gap.
  A program that writes \mintinline{python}{raise SystemExit(3)}
  constructs the exception itself, so neither the [[sys.exit]] wrapper
  nor the [[exit()]] stand-in sees it and the run is recorded as if it
  had ended normally.
  We could close it by rebinding [[SystemExit]] in [[builtins]] to a
  subclass of our own, but that would break every
  \mintinline{python}{except SystemExit} that catches a genuine
  [[SystemExit]] raised elsewhere, which is far worse than an
  occasional wrong exit code.
\end{description}


\section{Exit handler}

The [[on_exit]] function runs when the program terminates (via
[[atexit]]).
It restores the original streams---we must do this before running Git
commands, otherwise our own output would be captured recursively---and
then finalises the run in the repository.

We wrap everything in a [[try]]/[[except]] for the same reason as
[[initialize]]: learnlog must never cause an error at exit.
It routes to the same diagnostics path too, and this is the handler
where that matters most: an exception here loses the results of a run
that has already happened, and the surviving header is
indistinguishable from the one a [[SIGKILL]] leaves behind
(\cref{gitrepo}).
The [[errors.log]] entry is what tells those two apart afterwards.

