\section{Runs inside runs}

A logged program can start another logged program: a test runner
invoking a solution script, a build script running a compiler wrapper,
or simply [[subprocess.run]] in the student's own code.
Both processes record a run, and the inner one finishes first, so the
exit handlers interleave.
Nothing in this module coordinates them---the recorder in
\cref{gitrepo} makes each run address its own record instead
(\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}), which is what keeps the two apart.

Let's verify that end to end, since this is the case that used to merge
the two runs into one corrupted block: the child's output has to stay
with the child, the parent's with the parent, and both need their own
exit code.
The parent's own capture never sees the child's output, because the
child writes to the inherited file descriptor rather than through the
parent's [[sys.stdout]] wrapper.

Both processes run with [[site.py]] enabled, since the child is
started by the parent script rather than by the test, so a [[.pth]]
hook in the developer's own venv takes part in the run.
As in [[run_and_read_commit]] above, we settle the resulting ambiguity
by naming the project explicitly: what this test is about is that two
records stay apart, not where they are written.

