\section{Auto-starting without explicit import}
\label{sec:autostart}

The mechanism described so far captures everything \emph{after} the
interpreter reaches \mintinline{python}{import learnlog}.
Everything \emph{before} that line is invisible to us---and for
beginners, the commonest failure mode lives there.
A [[SyntaxError]] in [[main.py]] (a stray colon, a missing paren,
indentation that mixes tabs and spaces) aborts the interpreter
during \emph{compilation} of the script, before a single top-level
statement runs.
No hooks, no capture, nothing in the log.
Yet chasing syntax errors is a central debugging activity for
first-year students; omitting those runs leaves a visible gap in
both teaching and research data.

We need to attach \emph{before} CPython compiles the main script,
without changing how students launch their programs: [[python
main.py]], the VS Code \textbf{Run} button, and [[F5]] must
all keep working.
Python's [[site.py]] evaluates any line beginning with [[import]]
inside a [[.pth]] file at interpreter startup---ahead of the main
script and therefore ahead of parse errors in it.
So [[learnlog init]] installs a one-line
[[zzz-learnlog-autostart.pth]] into the venv's [[site-packages]]:
\begin{minted}{python}
import learnlog._autostart; learnlog._autostart.autostart()
\end{minted}

The [[_autostart]] module answers one question for the package:
\emph{which [[.learnlog/]] repository does a run in this interpreter
belong to?}
It answers it from the venv alone---a marker file and [[sys.prefix]],
neither of which depends on the current working directory---which is
what makes the answer stable no matter where the student launched the
program from.
[[initialize]] consults that answer as the second of its three
repository sources (\cref{sec:initialize}).
The [[autostart()]] entry point that the [[.pth]] line calls does
nothing more than import the package
(\cref{sec:autostart-on-import}).

\paragraph{Why [[.pth]] rather than [[sitecustomize.py]].}
The venv may already carry a [[sitecustomize.py]] written by
another tool (Python loads only one).
Multiple [[.pth]] files coexist, so we cannot collide.
The same trick powers [[coverage.py]]'s subprocess auto-start
([[coverage.pth]])---so the mechanism is proven at scale.
We also sort our hook late on purpose: editable installs often
drop a separate [[learnlog.pth]] that adds the source tree to
[[sys.path]], and that path must be visible before the hook tries
to import [[learnlog]].

\paragraph{Scope.}
After [[learnlog init]] the venv is the unit of logging: every
invocation of the venv's interpreter is captured.
Scripts anywhere on disk (including [[/tmp]]), the REPL,
[[python -c]], [[python -m foo]], [[pip]], [[pytest]], editor
linters that shell out to this interpreter, and every [[learnlog]]
subcommand \emph{except} [[learnlog init]] itself---all logged.
Students who want to work untracked step outside the venv.

\paragraph{What slips through.}
Python launched with [[-S]] (skips [[site.py]] and therefore
[[.pth]] files), embedded interpreters, other virtualenvs,
and any invocation with [[LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART=1]] in the
environment.
The opt-out variable is a deliberate escape hatch, and it is enforced
by [[initialize]] rather than by this hook
(\cref{sec:initialize})---so it silences the explicit
\mintinline{python}{import learnlog} path too.
Exporting it once covers a whole shell session, a grader's harness, or
a build step that should not appear in a student's log.


\subsection{Why this module must not import the package}
\label{sec:autostart-import-order}

The hook has one hard constraint that is invisible in its source and
easy to violate by accident, so it comes before the code.

\emph{Importing a submodule imports its package first.}
The [[.pth]] line says
\mintinline{python}{import learnlog._autostart}, and CPython satisfies
that by importing [[learnlog]], running [[learnlog/__init__.py]] to
completion---[[initialize]] and all---and only then executing
[[_autostart.py]].
The same happens one level down: a module-scope
\mintinline{python}{from learnlog import ...} anywhere in this file has
exactly the same effect at exactly the same moment.

Two consequences follow, and both were learned the hard way.

First, this module cannot decide \emph{whether} to log.
An earlier version put the whole activation gate in [[autostart()]]
and imported one helper from [[learnlog]] at module scope; every
invocation therefore started a run before the gate was consulted, and
the gate's answer changed nothing.
[[LEARNLOG_SKIP_AUTOSTART=1]] did not stop logging, and the project
root computed here was never the one used, so a venv's interpreter run
from an unrelated directory created a stray [[.learnlog/]] there and
committed whatever it found.
The decision now lives in [[initialize]] (\cref{sec:initialize}),
which runs early enough to be the only decision.

Second, the dependency between the two modules has to point one way:
[[learnlog/__init__.py]] imports from [[learnlog._autostart]], and
[[_autostart.py]] imports nothing but the standard library.
A back-import would reintroduce the problem, because it would run
during \emph{this} module's own import---and it would do so inside a
partially initialised package, where whether a name exists yet depends
on where it happens to sit in [[__init__.py]].
The stdlib-only rule is easy to check and impossible to satisfy
halfway.


\subsection{Shape of the module}

What is left is small: the project-root resolver, and an
[[autostart()]] function for the [[.pth]] line to call.
[[autostart()]] lives inside a bare [[try]]/[[except Exception:
pass]]: [[.pth]] files execute on \emph{every} Python startup,
including tools that have nothing to do with learnlog, so a
traceback here would break unrelated programs.

<<[[_autostart.py]]>>=
